One important thing about @RequestMapping
-------------------------------------------

We can actually use @RequestMapping instead of a specialized annotations

For example:

@RequestMapping(
    value="{/id}",
    method=RequestMethod.GET
)
public void getAllEmployeeById(@PathVariable int id){
...
}

This is equivalent to:

@GetMapping("/{id}")
public void getEmpById(@PathVariable int id){
...
}

Similarly,

@RequestMapping(
    value = {"/id"}
    method = RequestMethod.PUT
)
public void updateEmpById(@PathVariable int id, @RequestBody Employee employee){
...
}

This is equivalent to:

@PutMapping("/{id}")
public void updateEmployeeById(@PathVariable int id, @RequestBody Employee employee){
...
}

So:

@GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, and @DeleteMapping are specialized shortcuts for @RequestMapping.

Important Notes:
------------------

- @RequestMapping is used to map HTTP requests to controller classes or methods. It can specify the URL path,
  HTTP method, headers, parameters, and other request conditions.

- @RequestMapping is a general-purpose mapping annotation that can handle different HTTP methods, whereas
  @GetMapping specifically maps GET requests. @GetMapping is a composed shortcut for
  @RequestMapping(method = RequestMethod.GET)

- @PathVariable extracts a value from the URL path, such as /employees/101,
  whereas @RequestParam extracts a query parameter, such as /employees/search?department=IT

  @PathVariable is used when we want to identify specific data
  @RequestParam is used in operations like searching, sorting, filtering, pagination etc.

- @RequestBody tells Spring to read the HTTP request body and deserialize it into a Java object,
  typically from JSON

- We can use @PathVariable and @RequestBody together. Example: Update emp with id 101, JSON passed from body

