application.properties
-----------------------

application.properties is a configuration file used by Spring Boot to externalize application configuration
such as server port, database configuration, logging settings and custom application properties.
This allows us to change configuration without modifying the application code.

We have dependencies and automatic configuration. But applications still need configuration values.

For example:
server.port=8081

Where do we put it?
Usually:

src/main/resources/application.properties

Example

server.port=8081
spring.application.name=employee-service

Now our application runs on:
8081
instead of the default:
8080

Database example
We could configure:

spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/employees
spring.datasource.username=root
spring.datasource.password=password

Instead of hardcoding these inside Java.
We put it in: application.properties

This gives us:
Externalized configuration
Easier environment changes
Cleaner code
No need to modify Java code for configuration changes

application.yml file (YAML file)
--------------------------------

application.yml and application.properties solve the same basic problem.

They are two different formats for configuration.

properties -

server.port=8081
spring.application.name=employee-service

spring.datasource.url = jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/employeeDB
spring.datasource.username = root
spring.datasource.password = root
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update

yml -

server:
    port: 8081
spring:
    application:
        name: employee-service

spring:
    datasource:
        url: jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/employeeDB
        username: root
        password: root

    jpa:
        hibernate:
            ddl-auto: update

YAML is often easier to read for complex configuration.

Note: We can have both application.properties and application.yml file in our spring boot.
      Both are supported configuration formats, but typically a project follows one format consistently.

Profiles
---------

Profiles allow us to maintain environment-specific configuration. For example, we can have separate configurations
for DEV, UAT and PROD while keeping the application code the same. We activate the appropriate profile during deployment.

Imagine our application has three environments:

DEV
UAT
PROD

Our database will be different in each environment.

DEV:
dev-db

UAT:
uat-db

PROD:
prod-db

We don't want to modify Java code every time we deploy.
That's exactly why Profiles exist.

Example
We can create:

application.properties
application-dev.properties
application-uat.properties
application-prod.properties

application.properties has Common configuration:

spring.application.name=employee-service

application-dev.properties
server.port=8081
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://dev-db:3306/employees

application-uat.properties
server.port=8082
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://uat-db:3306/employees

application-prod.properties
server.port=8080
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://prod-db:3306/employees

Now we activate the required profile.

For example:
spring.profiles.active=de

Spring Boot loads:
application.properties
+
application-dev.properties

Same application code.
Different configuration.
That's the beauty of profiles.

Ways to activate a profile
---------------------------

1 - through application.properties file - spring.profiles.active = dev
2 - thorough command line - java -jar employee.jar --spring.profiles.active=prod

In real deployments, the profile is often supplied externally rather than hardcoded.

Spring Boot Actuator
----------------------

Spring Boot Actuator provides production-ready monitoring and management capabilities for Spring Boot applications.
It exposes endpoints such as /actuator/health and /actuator/metrics, which help us monitor the application health, metrics
and runtime information.

Why do we need Actuators
---------------------------
Imagine our Java application is running in production.

We want to know:
Is application UP?
How much memory is being used?
Are my endpoints working?
What beans are loaded?
What metrics are available?

Instead of logging into the server and investigating everything manually, Actuator exposes monitoring endpoints.

Adding Actuator
-----------------

XML:
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

Then we can access actuator endpoints.

One of the most important:
/actuator/health

For example:
http://localhost:8080/actuator/health

Response could be:
JSON
{
  "status": "UP"
}

Important Actuator endpoints
-----------------------------

/actuator/health
Tells whether application is healthy

/actuator/metrics
Provides application metrics - for example - JVM memory, CPU, HTTP requests, etc.

/actuator/bean
Shows Spring beans.

/actuator/info
Provides application information if configured.

/actuator/mappings
Shows request mappings.
Very useful when debugging.

We don't normally expose every actuator endpoint publicly.

For example, exposing:
/actuator/beans
/actuator/env
could reveal sensitive application information.

So production applications generally restrict access to actuator endpoints.

Suppose our application is deployed and users complain:
"The application is not working."

We can check:
/actuator/health

If:

{
    "status": "UP"
}

the application itself is running.

If database health is included and we see something like:

{
  "status": "DOWN"
}

with a DB-related error, that gives us a strong clue that the problem may be with the database/connectivity.

